    dict(key='28-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n  THE XML FINDINGS, WORKED OUT (a bank that parses no XML):\n    findings   5 mention XML\n    one        INP19 XML External Entities Blowup, 2x\n    condition  usesXMLParser is False or disablesDTD is False\n    usesXMLParser is False      -> True\n    disablesDTD is False        -> True\n    True or True = True: reported\n    a person   reads it and says: does not apply\n  WHAT pytm DOES NOT HOLD:\n    no owner, no accept or transfer, no review date\n    no check that a mitigation exists: the answers are claims\n    so the register of the plain project stays',
         narration='The X M L findings, worked out. [[slnc 300]] The condition is true because the bank uses no X M L parser. [[slnc 300]] A person reads it and says it does not apply. [[slnc 400]] And what pytm does not hold: no owner, no decision, no review date, and no check that a control exists. [[slnc 300]] So the register of decisions stays.'),
    dict(key='29-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['Each answer is a claim, and can be untrue', 'A generic threat library gives candidates', 'The model is a second copy of the design', 'pytm keeps no decisions or owners', 'It needs Python and Graphviz'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] Each answer is a claim, and an untrue one quietly hides a finding. [[slnc 500]] A generic threat library gives candidates, and some will not apply, like X M L threats in a bank that parses none. [[slnc 500]] The model is a second copy of the design, which must be kept in step with it. [[slnc 500]] pytm keeps no owner, no decision, and no review date. [[slnc 300]] And it needs Python and Graphviz installed.'),
